---
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from 'astrowind:config';
import JsonLd from '~/components/common/JsonLd.astro';

const metadata = {
  title: `Beginner's Guide to SUP Paddling — ${SITE.name}`,
  description:
    'Step-by-step guide for first-time SUP paddlers. Learn stance, stroke technique, turning, safety checks, and how to launch.',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <JsonLd
    type="Article"
    article={{
      headline: "Beginner's Guide to SUP Paddling",
      description:
        'Step-by-step guide for first-time SUP paddlers. Learn stance, stroke technique, turning, safety checks, and how to launch.',
      datePublished: '2025-01-15',
    }}
    data={{ url: 'https://paddleboardoverstock.com/guides/beginner-guide' }}
  />
  <JsonLd
    type="HowTo"
    howto={{
      name: "Beginner's Guide to SUP Paddling",
      description: 'Step-by-step guide for first-time SUP paddlers.',
      steps: [
        {
          name: 'Choose the Right Board',
          text: 'Start with a wide, stable board like the Paddle Board Overstock SUP Explorer 11 with 32" width for a stable platform.',
        },
        {
          name: 'Safety First',
          text: 'Always wear a life vest (PFD). Attach the leash to your ankle. Check weather and wind conditions before launching.',
        },
        {
          name: 'Basic Stance',
          text: 'Stand with feet shoulder-width apart, parallel to the carrying handle. Keep knees slightly bent. Look at the horizon.',
        },
        {
          name: 'The Forward Stroke',
          text: 'Reach forward with the paddle blade, submerge it fully, and pull it back alongside the board. Switch sides every 5-10 strokes.',
        },
        {
          name: 'Turning',
          text: 'For a sweep turn reach wide to the side and sweep in a half-circle toward the tail. For a reverse turn paddle backward on one side.',
        },
        {
          name: 'Getting Back On',
          text: 'If you fall, stay with your board. Swim to the side, place your paddle on the board, and use a kick to push yourself up.',
        },
      ],
    }}
  />
  <section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto py-24 px-6" data-ai-block="definition">
    <a href="/guides" class="text-sm text-sky-600 hover:text-sky-700">→Guides</a>
    <h1 class="mt-4 text-4xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Beginner's Guide to Paddling</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-gray-500">Everything you need to know before your first time on the water.</p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">1. Choose the Right Board</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
          Start with a wide, stable board. The Paddle Board Overstock SUP Explorer 11 has a 32" width that provides a
          stable platform for first-time paddlers. Wider boards are more forgiving while you learn balance.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">2. Safety First</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
          Always wear a life vest (PFD). Attach the leash to your ankle. Check weather and wind conditions before
          launching. Start on calm, flat water — lakes and sheltered bays are ideal.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">3. Basic Stance</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
          Stand with feet shoulder-width apart, parallel to the carrying handle. Keep knees slightly bent. Look at the
          horizon, not your feet. Your core does the work, not your arms.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">4. The Forward Stroke</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
          Reach forward with the paddle blade, submerge it fully, and pull it back alongside the board. Switch sides
          every 5-10 strokes. Use your torso rotation — not just your arms — for power.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">5. Turning</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
          For a sweep turn: reach wide to the side and sweep in a half-circle toward the tail. For a reverse turn:
          paddle backward on one side. Practice both in open water before navigating tight spots.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">6. Getting Back On</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
          If you fall, stay with your board. Swim to the side, place your paddle on the board, and use a kick to push
          yourself up onto the deck in one motion. Keep your weight low.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Common Beginner Mistakes</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
        Most beginner frustration comes from a handful of fixable habits. Looking down at your feet instead of the
        horizon shifts your weight forward and destroys balance. Standing too far forward or too far back makes the
        board plow or wander — center your feet around the carrying handle. Pulling with your arms alone tires you
        quickly; the power should come from torso rotation, with arms acting as the connection between core and paddle.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
        Beyond technique, two setup mistakes cause most first-session failures: ignoring the wind forecast and launching
        in an exposed spot. Choose a sheltered area for your first outings, and always attach the leash before you step
        on the board — not after you are already on the water.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-gray-900">Your First Session: What to Expect</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
        The board will feel tippy for the first ten minutes — this is normal, not a sign you chose the wrong board. Your
        stabilizing muscles are simply learning a new task. Start on your knees if standing feels unstable, then
        progress to a standing stance once you are away from shore. Keep the first session to 30-45 minutes; fatigue
        arrives faster than beginners expect, and tired paddlers fall more.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-gray-600">
        Expect some soreness in your core, shoulders, and lower legs the next day. That is the workout working. By the
        second or third session, the wobble disappears and paddling starts to feel natural.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 p-6 bg-sky-50 rounded-2xl border border-sky-100">
      <h2 class="font-semibold text-gray-900">Related</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-3 text-sm">
        <a
          href="/guides/how-to-choose-your-sup"
          class="px-3 py-1.5 bg-white rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-gray-700 hover:border-sky-300"
          >How to Choose Your SUP</a
        >
        <a
          href="/guides/inflatable-vs-hard"
          class="px-3 py-1.5 bg-white rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-gray-700 hover:border-sky-300"
          >Inflatable vs Hard Board</a
        >
        <a
          href="/guides/safety-tips"
          class="px-3 py-1.5 bg-white rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-gray-700 hover:border-sky-300"
          >Safety Tips</a
        >
        <a
          href="/products"
          class="px-3 py-1.5 bg-white rounded-lg border border-gray-200 text-gray-700 hover:border-sky-300"
          >Shop Boards</a
        >
      </div>
    </section>
  </section>
</Layout>
